"language": {"code": "oj", "name": "Ojibwe"}, "word": "joka"}, {"language": {"code": "fa-ira", "name": "Iranian Persian"}, "word": "جوکِر"}, {"language": {"code": "pl", "name": "Polish"}, "word": "joker"}, {"language": {"code": "pl", "name": "Polish"}, "word": "dżoker"}, {"language": {"code": "pt", "name": "Portuguese"}, "word": "curinga"}, {"language": {"code": "pt", "name": "Portuguese"}, "word": "jóquer m melé"}, {"language": {"code": "ro", "name": "Romanian"}, "word": "joker"}, {"language": {"code": "ru", "name": "Russian"}, "word": "джо́кер"}, {"language": {"code": "sh", "name": "Serbo-Croatian"}, "word": "џо̀кер"}, {"language": {"code": "sh", "name": "Serbo-Croatian"}, "word": "džòker"}, {"language": {"code": "sk", "name": "Slovak"}, "word": "žolík"}, {"language": {"code": "sl", "name": "Slovene"}, "word": "džoker"}, {"language": {"code": "es", "name": "Spanish"}, "word": "comodín"}, {"language": {"code": "sv", "name": "Swedish"}, "word": "joker"}, {"language": {"code": "th", "name": "Thai"}, "word": "โจ๊กเกอร์"}, {"language": {"code": "th", "name": "Thai"}, "word": "ไพ่โจ๊กเกอร์"}, {"language": {"code": "tr", "name": "Turkish"}, "word": "joker"}, {"language": {"code": "uk", "name": "Ukrainian"}, "word": "джо́кер"}, {"language": {"code": "ur", "name": "Urdu"}, "word": "جوکَر"}], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(figurative) Something kept in reserve that can be used to gain an advantage; a trump card.", "tags": ["figuratively"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "Rebus decided to use their joker. 'Mr Lintz made a phone call to this office. He was talking for over twenty minutes.'", "reference": "2008 September 18, Ian Rankin, The Hanging Garden: The #1 bestselling series that inspired BBC One’s REBUS, Hachette UK, →ISBN:"}, {"text": "Tash Wilson played her 'joker' , pulling her chair so close our legs were almost touching. 'Hey, Chris, do you want to go out sometime? All you've got to do is say the word.'", "reference": "2012 January 1, Simon Packham, Silenced, Bonnier Publishing Fiction Ltd., →ISBN:"}, {"text": "The director made me understand that my bubble of stardom could burst just as soon as it had been inflated if I didn't watch my attitude. Then he used his joker. I had an understudy so if I messed up or tried to create a scene, I would be summarily replaced.", "reference": "2016 October 17, Dipo Adesida, My French Teacher Had an Igbo Accent: An Inspiring Collection of Memories, Memoirs and Mischiefs, Partridge Africa, →ISBN:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "An unspecified, vaguely disreputable person.", "tags": [], "examples": ["Some joker keeps throwing eggs at my windows."], "quotes": [], "synonyms": ["clown"], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": [{"definition": "A loser.", "tags": [], "examples": ["Don't waste your breath on these jokers round here."], "quotes": [], "synonyms": ["clown"], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}]}, {"definition": "(New Zealand, colloquial) A man.", "tags": ["New Zealand", "colloquial"], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "A clause in a contract that undermines its apparent provisions.", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "Discussion of contracts and the many provisions contained therein led to a vote making it the sense of the convention that manufacturers should use a simple sales contract, free from jokers.", "reference": "1922, Farm Machinery and Equipment, page lxxxiii:"}, {"text": "Then, sir, on page 12 of the agreement there is a joker clause, which provides for payments in addition to the ten per cent, […]", "reference": "1939, Canadian Parliament, Official Report of Debates, House of Commons, volume 218, page 858:"}, {"text": "Stone claimed that there was a Joker in the contract, one clause (No. 2) calling for two weeks' notice and another (No. 8) calling for payment on a par-day basis after the first two weeks.", "reference": "1942, Billboard, volume 54, number 41, page 5:"}, {"text": "The object of these provisions is to prevent insertion of \"jokers\" or \"sleepers\" in bills and securing passage under the false color of the title.", "reference": "1958, Duncan Leroy Kennedy, Bill drafting, page 12:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(military) A friendly unit that acts as a suspected hostile unit in a military excercise.", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "Joker - A friendly track or contact acting as a \"suspect\" track for exercise purposes only. (STANAG 1241)", "reference": "1998, APP-6A: Military Symbols for Land-Based Systems, page 9:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "The option, in a pub quiz, of selecting one particular round in which one's team will score double points.", "tags": [], "examples": ["We used our joker as soon as the topic of sports was announced, since we are sport experts."], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(chess) A fairy chess piece that moves like the last piece that was moved by the opponent.", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "The problem is that it would be difficult to use a joker offensively since you could never know how it was going to move, meaning it’d be most useful setting it up for potential queen/rook captures to disincentivize your opponent from moving those pieces.", "reference": "2018 October 12, aabicus, “My 7 Favorite Fairy Chess Pieces”, in The Daily SPUF:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": ["buffoon", "clown", "comedian", "comic", "cutup", "funnyman", "gagman", "gagster", "giber", "humorist", "jackpudding", "joker", "jokester", "wag", "wit"], "antonyms": []}], "source": {"url": "https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/joker", "license": {"name": "CC BY-SA 4.0